Beryllium: on FreeBSD with NVIDIA, finish a frame before Wayland shows it

Purpose: the owner saw the browser window flicker when moving the mouse
  (FreeBSD 16.0-CURRENT, NVIDIA RTX 5090 Laptop, driver 595.99.02, KDE
  Plasma Wayland). On Wayland, Chromium draws each frame into a GBM buffer
  and hands the buffer to the compositor (GbmSurfacelessWayland). The
  compositor must not read the buffer before the GPU has finished drawing
  it. Three ways to ensure that exist, and on this machine none is in use:
  1. Explicit sync, an acquire fence sent with the buffer: KWin here offers
     neither zwp_linux_explicit_synchronization_v1 nor
     wp_linux_drm_syncobj_manager_v1 (beryllium://gpu, "Interfaces exposed
     by the Wayland compositor"), and the imported BSD patches turn acquire
     fences and the sync_file interop off on BSD
     (patch-ui_ozone_platform_wayland_host_wayland__frame__manager.cc,
     patch-ui_ozone_platform_wayland_host_wayland__buffer__manager__host.cc).
  2. A CPU wait on the frame's GPU fence before committing: Chromium does
     this when the compositor takes no fences, but the fences are file
     descriptors from EGL_ANDROID_native_fence_sync, whose code is not built
     on BSD (patch-ui_gl_BUILD.gn excludes gl_fence_android_native_fence_sync
     and gl_fence.cc enables it only for Linux, ChromeOS and Android). The
     frame has no fences, so the wait does nothing and the buffer is
     committed right after glFlush().
  3. Implicit sync in the kernel: Mesa drivers attach their rendering to
     the buffer, so the compositor's reads wait for it. NVIDIA's driver does
     not.
  So with NVIDIA the compositor can show a frame that is still being drawn;
  repaints from hovering and moving the mouse make that visible as flicker.
  On FreeBSD, when the compositor takes no acquire fence and the GL driver
  is NVIDIA's, the GPU process now calls glFinish() instead of glFlush()
  before committing, so every committed buffer is complete. That costs the
  GPU thread the time the GPU needs for the frame; Mesa drivers keep
  glFlush(). The driver is recognised by the GL vendor string (FreeBSD
  reports PCI vendor 0x0000). The existing "rely on implicit sync" driver
  workaround still skips the wait. For comparison,
  --disable-features=BerylliumWaylandWaitForGpu turns it off.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on the imported BSD patches named
  above (stage 2).
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench.
Known gap: a proper fix is explicit sync (sync_file or DRM syncobj
  support on FreeBSD for NVIDIA, and KWin offering
  wp_linux_drm_syncobj_manager_v1 there); then this wait is not needed.
Test: manual (no automated check sees flicker): move the mouse over tabs
  and toolbar buttons with and without
  --disable-features=BerylliumWaylandWaitForGpu.
security-review: not required (GPU synchronisation only; no new input
  handling)

--- a/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/gpu/gbm_surfaceless_wayland.cc
+++ b/ui/ozone/platform/wayland/gpu/gbm_surfaceless_wayland.cc
